  "Welcome back to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall. Today we enter The Human Communication Layer & Inclusion. Before code synthesis models and automated agents, developers met face to face to establish standards of craftsmanship. Today we rewatch Episode 15: Jen Myers from SCNA 2012 (2012).",
  "Jen Myers was a leading voice in Chicago tech, speaking at SCNA on why craftsmanship must include user experience, empathy, and welcoming adult career changers.",
  "What inspired Jen Myers to bridge the cultural divide between visual designers and software craftsmen? How did Girl Develop It create safe spaces for adult learners in Chicago? What principles guided Jen Myers when this conversation was recorded? How do those lessons inform the software systems we design today?",
  "Roll archive footage (YouTube ID: en6xSNFYNLE). Watch for the unvarnished hallway exchanges, the emphasis on craft over speed, and the sheer enthusiasm of engineers taking responsibility for their software.",
  "Software without user empathy is dead machinery. In an AI world, user experience design and humane interaction design matter more than raw backend plumbing. When implementation is cheap, verification and clarity become everything. We cannot accept the mirage of competence. Let us examine the terminal contracts directly.",
  "Thank you for joining today's session. Full transcripts, provenance notes, and companion research are available at just3ws.com. In our next broadcast, we explore Episode 16. Until next time, keep your feedback loops fast and your code humane."
]